Linear sound wave (3D)
----------------------

A plane sound wave of relative amplitude A = 1e-3 along x in a periodic unit
box filled with a 3D glass (32^3 particles, i.e. 32 particles per wavelength
for one wavelength across the box). rho0 = 1 and P0 = 1 / gamma give a sound
speed of 1 and a period of 1. The density perturbation is imposed by the
Lagrangian displacement of the glass particles, the pressure follows the
adiabatic relation.

Two variants (makeIC.py options):

  travelling wave (default): v_x = A cs cos kx. The complex amplitude of the
      k mode, Z(t) = (2 / M) sum_i m_i v_x,i exp(-i k x_i), rotates at the
      frequency cs k with a constant modulus: the measured phase speed gives
      the numerical dispersion error, the decay of |Z| the damping.
  --standing: v = 0. The mode oscillates in place; the energy of the mode,
      |Z_v|^2 + cs^2 |Z_rho|^2, is constant for the exact solution and its
      decay over many periods gives the damping rate. Run it longer
      (time_end 10) with the suite.

--nwave n puts n wavelengths across the box (32 / n particles per wavelength),
--boost adds a bulk velocity (Galilean invariance). The amplitude 1e-3 keeps
the wave linear for ~100 periods (the shock-formation time is
2 / ((gamma + 1) A cs k)); the mode projection averages the glass noise out.

plotSolution.py reads all snapshots and prints the measured phase speed,
frequency and damping rate per period.

The code needs to be compiled with --with-hydro-dimension=3 (the default).
